H2: Known Faults - Fiat 500
H3: Dualogic Gearbox
P: MONITOR ú Some 500 models ú Get a quote
P: WhoCanFixMyCar says the Dualogic automated manual transmission in some 500 models is notoriously problematic, and lists gearbox and Dualogic automatic faults among the repeat trouble spots of the car. If the car you are looking at has this gearbox, ask for its service and repair history and take a long test drive.
H3: Power Steering Torque Sensor
P: MONITOR ú Reported across the 500 ú Get a quote
P: According to WhoCanFixMyCar, a faulty torque sensor in the steering column is one of the most common causes of power steering failure in the Fiat 500. Check that the steering feels the same in both directions and that no steering warning light stays on.
H3: Clutch
P: MONITOR ú Petrol 500s, ex-driving-school cars ú Get a quote
P: Auto Express says premature clutch failure isn't rare with petrol 500s. The RAC adds that many will have been used as driving school cars, so you should check for slipping clutches. On a test drive, pull away on a hill and accelerate in a high gear to see whether the revs rise without the car gaining speed.
H3: Door Handles
P: MINOR ú Reported across the 500 ú Get a quote
P: Door handles come up in several reviews. WhoCanFixMyCar calls the exterior door handle hinge a known weak spot that can break and make the handle difficult to use. Auto Express says the seat-tipping mechanism and door handles can be trouble, with the handles a particular weakness, and the RAC says the driver's door handle has been known to break easily. Try every handle from inside and outside, and tip the front seats.
H3: Interior Trim and Paint
P: MINOR ú Reported across the 500 ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer says fragile interior trim can snap off or crack, and that soft paint is easily marked by bird mess or stone chips. The RAC has also come across trim rattles. Press on the dashboard and door trims with the radio off, and look over the paint in good light.
H3: Warning Lights and Infotainment
P: MONITOR ú Reported across the range ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says earlier petrol models were praised for their general dependability, but faulty dashboard warning lights and glitchy infotainment screens cropped up across the range. WhoCanFixMyCar reports owner feedback highlighting incorrect warning lights and infotainment problems as recurring electrical issues, and the RAC mentions problems with the heating and ventilation system. Check that every warning light goes out after the engine starts, and try the screen, heater and fan.
H3: TwinAir Oil Weep and Economy
P: MINOR ú 0.9 TwinAir ú Get a quote
P: On cars with the 0.9 TwinAir engine, WhoCanFixMyCar says oil can weep from the timing cover area due to sealing issues, which may leave oily patches under the front of the engine or a burning oil smell. Auto Express tested the 104bhp TwinAir and said no TwinAir gets close to its claimed figures: the claim was 67.3mpg and its test average was 38.7mpg. Look under the front of the engine for oil.
H3: Electric 500 Electrical Glitches
P: MINOR ú Electric 500 ú Get a quote
P: By electric standards, What Car? says the Fiat 500e scored slightly above average marks in its 2025 Reliability Survey, and that the most common glitches were electrical issues that weren't related to the electric motor. WhoCanFixMyCar describes these as software glitches, screen or infotainment problems and warning messages. What Car? adds that the battery is covered by a separate eight-year warranty, capped at 100,000 miles.
P: The electric car has its own page: see our Fiat 500e common problems guide .
